			<div class="content" id="post-content-4831494">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre (Optional):</strong> Horror with a tinge of dark comedy.</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout (Optional):</strong> A standard SCP article included with experiences of D-Class dealing with the SCP and footnotes of some unusual instances of the SCP</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> The first instance of SCP-??? is a small gnat that appears in the affected individual’s room anywhere from 10 P.M to 2&#160;A.M and is first seen flying around a light source in the room or heard buzzing past the individual’s ear. This gnat proves extremely annoying, being nearly impossible to catch and contain for the night and will buzz by the individual’s ear just as they’re falling asleep. However, the gnat is very easy to kill, seemingly stopping if it’s approached with the intent of swatting it. But, killing the gnat will activate the next “stage” of SCP-???, this time a fly. Killing this instance again “ramps” up the SCP to a mosquito the next night, and so on and so on until you get mosquitoes with malaria, murder hornets, and other terrible crawlies. However, no matter what stage you’re on, they all follow the same rule of the insect being able to fly around and of being easy to kill but nearly impossible to catch and contain.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> I want to focus on how D-Class subjects either cope or go insane from this terrible predicament, and how bad it can get if you keep ramping up the stages.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> A crawly in your rooms at night is something we’ve all dealt with at some point, so I feel like that familiar premise combined with my unique (hopefully) idea activates that deep fear of insects in the reader.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> I’m not positive if I want to have it ramp up to the point of being unbearable, or plateau at a certain point so it’s not enough to be completely devastating, but the annoyance is enough to slowly eat away at the individual. Curious on what your thoughts are.</p>

							</div>

								
					
